WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.972
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.535 --> 00:00:07.910
BRIAN DORSEY:
We're going to talk

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.050
about how to make sense of
all of our compute pieces,

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.430
where to run your
stuff, give hopefully

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.833
a good lay of the land
of where things are at.

00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.500
I was talking with
folks ahead of time--

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.862
a bunch of data science
related folks in the room.

00:00:19.862 --> 00:00:22.070
I'm not going to talk about
anything machine learning

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.500
or data science directly,
but about the platforms that

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.790
run a lot of that
stuff on our system.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.923
So might be useful there.

00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:30.590
Let's get going.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.560
So the key question.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.020
Where should I run my code?

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.430
And unfortunately, the
answer is it depends.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.100
Thus we have a
whole talk about it.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.670
So let's get into it.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.740
At a really high
level, I'm going

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.680
to talk about five
different products.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.600
And I was talking with folks.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.740
I think half the challenge in
getting started in Google Cloud

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.812
is actually just so many
names of all of the things.

00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:02.270
There's like a
couple hundred names

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.853
you need to learn just to kind
of make sense of all the parts.

00:01:04.853 --> 00:01:08.390
So these are the names we're
going to talk about today.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.790
And starting at the top, we
have Cloud Functions, which

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:13.820
is functions as a service.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.270
App Engine, which is
for taking a set of code

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.900
and running that as
a website, usually.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.520
Cloud Run, which
is new, and we'll

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.970
talk about that in details--

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.130
announced today.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:31.280
Kubernetes Engine, which is
a hosted Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.745
And Compute Engine, which
are virtual machines, network

00:01:34.745 --> 00:01:37.550
and disk, running there.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:42.030
So just out of curiosity, how
people heard of containers?

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:42.530
Sorry.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.060
Thank you for
raising your hands.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.346
And serverless?

00:01:46.346 --> 00:01:48.490
And everybody has a
different definition.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:49.490
We have 100 people here.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:53.190
We probably have 100 definitions
of serverless is my guess.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:57.110
So my operating
general definition

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:02.960
here is mostly around the kind
of operational model of you

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.540
pay for the actual
usage you get.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.800
The infrastructure
itself is managed.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.870
You focus on your code,
that sort of thing.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.660
There's another angle
as well, though,

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.400
that I think is interesting.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.930
And we usually take
these directly together.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:22.400
And there are cases where we can
tease these apart a little bit.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.870
It's the programming
model itself,

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.672
like what it looks like
from a developer standpoint.

00:02:26.672 --> 00:02:28.130
And most things
that are serverless

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:32.725
are not actually storing state
between function calls, right?

00:02:32.725 --> 00:02:34.850
You have side effects of
your code or return values

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.430
of the functions.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.603
Usually kind of
a hosted service.

00:02:39.603 --> 00:02:42.020
Event-driven is a word I'm
going to talk about a bit more.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.350
But usually there's something
that causes it to happen.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.630
A request comes in
or an event fires.

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.188
And we're trying to make
these more and more open

00:02:50.188 --> 00:02:51.980
so that you can run
them in lots of places.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.080
And I'll talk about that a bit.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.270
So the key question here
is, there's so many choices.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.830
Am I going to make
the right decision?

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.933
Kind of decision
paralysis sort of thing.

00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:06.600
And I get there.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.830
And my goal for the talk
is really two things.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.390
We kind of get a big
map of the territory

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.410
about what these pieces
are and relax a little bit

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.670
about the risk here.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.150
I'm going to try to
make the case that it's

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.430
fairly easy to move
around after you've

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.020
made a starting decision.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.680
So no worries.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.050
And at a really
high level, and this

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.570
is the kind of able
to move up and down.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.900
The top two, you're mostly
focusing as a developer

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.730
on the code you write--

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:44.720
the actual function signature,
what returns from it,

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.450
or the URL maps
to different parts

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.550
of the code, that sort of thing.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.680
And down here,
we're mostly focused

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.410
on building a
runtime environment

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.790
with the right
binaries, the files

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.985
it needs, the container
of the code itself.

00:04:00.985 --> 00:04:02.360
And then at Compute
Engine, we're

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.010
looking at a whole
operating system.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.540
They're virtual machines.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.915
You can install
anything you want

00:04:06.915 --> 00:04:08.490
in them, that sort of deal.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:13.310
So just going to go all
the way through all of them

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.899
and move kind of quick, and
then circle back around.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:21.730
So Cloud Functions.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.710
When you're using
Cloud Functions,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.580
you are writing
just one function,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.580
and it maps usually to a
URL, but some kind of event

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.540
that comes in.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.960
And as a developer, you just
think about what inputs, what

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.145
side effects, and what outputs.

00:04:36.145 --> 00:04:37.770
And that's the whole
world while you're

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.450
thinking about that function.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.650
The events can come from
a lot of different places.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.880
It's very common for them to
come in as an HTTP request.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.100
But in cloud-- more words--

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.830
Pub/Sub is our messaging bus.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.643
Cloud Storage is
an object store.

00:04:55.643 --> 00:04:57.310
And there's a few
other options as well,

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.610
like hooks to
Firebase and the like.

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.820
And these are fully managed.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.100
You just write the
code, hit Deploy,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.420
and it scales up and down.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:11.040
And you pay for exactly
what you actually use,

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.750
nothing more, nothing less.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:16.225
And it auto scales
with actual usage.

00:05:16.225 --> 00:05:17.850
So the idea is,
hopefully, you can just

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.950
concentrate on that
one part of the world

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.627
and not worry about
anything else.

00:05:21.627 --> 00:05:23.460
So when you're using
these, you are thinking

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.320
about doing system design.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:26.700
You're thinking
about what events

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:31.540
are going to cause the function
to execute and what it does.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.770
So that's the mental
space you're in when

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.490
you're working with Functions.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.490
And it's the classic
definition of serverless,

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.900
in a way, so it's a good fit
for things that are serverless.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.630
When you're making integrations
between other pieces,

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:50.810
events are coming from
Pub/Sub or Storage,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.510
you can set things up so you
send a new object to a Cloud

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:55.250
Storage bucket and
that causes a function

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:56.667
to fire that does
something to it,

00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.670
maybe makes a different
version of it, copies it

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.910
somewhere else,
that sort of thing.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.640
And it's used sometimes
in ETL processes

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.650
or transactional things where
you need to, for example,

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.070
the storage example-- every
time a new item goes in,

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.712
you do something to it.

00:06:12.712 --> 00:06:14.420
And then my favorite
kind of mental model

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:18.290
is just thinking of these
are the glue of the web.

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:23.410
This is the HTTP glue that
connects everything together.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.520
On the constraint side, you
are seeing the entire world

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.630
through this
function granularity.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.960
So as you build more
complicated systems,

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:36.380
you piece together more
functions to do that.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:37.987
And that works pretty well.

00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:40.570
And then at a certain point, it
might be hard to reason about.

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:44.650
Or it might be more challenging
to monitor or troubleshoot.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:47.110
And so pay attention to that.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.400
And in Cloud Functions, there
are only specific runtimes

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:51.920
available.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.050
So if you're running in
those languages, awesome.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.990
If you're not, you might
not find them as useful.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.790
So what does this look like.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.160
Let's see.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.970
Look at Cloud Functions.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:07.550
Actually, let me just go
to the top a little bit.

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:11.140
This is the Google
Cloud Platform web UI

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.470
looking at a specific project.

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.420
And in my opinion, this is the
single most important thing,

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:20.900
the hamburger button, because
it gets to all the other pieces.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:25.400
If you don't find that, it's
really hard to do anything.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.090
But if we come in and
look at functions,

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.307
I've got several
functions set up.

00:07:30.307 --> 00:07:32.890
And let's go ahead and use Node
because I think a lot of folks

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:37.420
are using Node JavaScript
based functions.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.120
And this one's already built.
So it's got some source code.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:44.200
And all it's really
doing is saying,

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.450
for every request that
comes in, let's look

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:49.345
for some stuff in the message
body, or in the request,

00:07:49.345 --> 00:07:50.810
or in the body.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:53.170
And if I don't find that,
just use hello world

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:55.000
and return that back
out to the function.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:59.680
So this is kind of hello
world of functions.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:04.510
And it automatically
gets a URL, and you

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:07.780
can map additional ones.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:14.420
But if we run this, we
get the default input.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.005
OK.

00:08:15.005 --> 00:08:16.630
But I wanted to do
something different.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.510
So there's this testing
window over here.

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:22.000
So we can actually
kind of simulate

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.580
giving it a body in a POST
request as part of the post.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:35.919
And so we go message, and
hello lovely Next peoples.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:38.260
That's you.

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.929
And we run the function, and
we get a different output.

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:44.440
So yay, proof that it's real.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.260
Additionally, we
can view the logs

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.360
because the actual
execution logs

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.537
and any log messages you
emit during the function call

00:08:52.537 --> 00:08:53.620
go to Stackdriver Logging.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.650
So you can see them all in a
central place and the like.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:02.380
So it's a very, very quick taste
of what that can feel like.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.900
And of course, you can also do
that through the command line

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:06.970
or via APIs as well.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:08.530
Just kind of the
web interface to it.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:14.330
So why would you
pick Cloud Functions?

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.220
So a big part is you don't
think about the infrastructure

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.220
of the servers at all,
like classic serverless.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:23.700
And this granularity of payment.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:26.270
So if you have things that are
very spiky or you don't run

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.000
very often, they will
be extremely afford--

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.500
like inexpensive--

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.330
I'm trying to avoid
saying "cheap"--

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.580
they'll be inexpensive
all the way,

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:36.200
if you're not running
them very often.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.020
And they can scale
up to lots of load.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.720
And it's a very straightforward
developer experience.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.700
You've got your
code and it runs.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:49.220
Deep breath.

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.450
And now App Engine.

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.080
So App Engine takes
the code focus

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.520
but goes out as a level to
think about a whole application.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:01.535
So we're talking about
usually a web application

00:10:01.535 --> 00:10:04.160
because that's the sweet
spot for App Engine.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.040
And so you'll have your
URL mappings of this URL

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.300
calls this code, this
URL calls that code.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.910
Maybe there's some shared code.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:13.910
There's a whole structure
of the application

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.300
that's all one piece.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.900
So that's kind of the way
you see the world when

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.090
using App Engine.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:22.130
Again, no service to manage.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.662
It scales up and
down really fast.

00:10:24.662 --> 00:10:26.120
But it has some
additional features

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.718
like versioning
of the code base.

00:10:28.718 --> 00:10:30.260
And then you can do
traffic splitting

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:31.603
across those versions.

00:10:31.603 --> 00:10:33.270
So as you do a new
release, you can say,

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:35.390
oh, I'd like to give a
small amount of traffic

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:36.590
to the new release.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:40.280
And then-- whoops-- roll that
back, that sort of thing,

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.350
if it doesn't work out.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:50.770
App Engine has existed longer
than Google Cloud itself has,

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:51.490
actually.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.830
It predates GCP.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.910
So it actually has three
different execution

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:58.480
environments.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.780
There's a classic kind
of Standard Edition,

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:05.140
and there's our newer
second gen runtimes.

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:06.700
And additionally,
you can actually

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.980
use the same view of
the world and have

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.350
the code deployed to
VMs, and that's called

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.370
the flexible environment.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:18.860
So there are some differences
between these environments.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.870
If you're starting
new projects, I highly

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.750
recommend go straight for
the second gen pieces.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:30.380
If you need to, for example,
if you need to listen on ports

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:31.880
to do web sockets
or something other

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:38.600
than HTTP, other
than short HTTP,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.030
you can do that on the VMs.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:42.920
If you need certain
machine sizes,

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:45.170
like if you need a really
particular big machine size,

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:46.340
you might go to flex.

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:49.850
But by and large, people are
running in the second gen

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:53.630
standard nowadays.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:59.100
And in this space, the runtimes
are actually open source.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.653
They're written to be idiomatic.

00:12:00.653 --> 00:12:02.570
They should feel like
the programming language

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:03.173
you're using.

00:12:03.173 --> 00:12:05.090
So it should feel like
Python to Python people

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:08.570
and feel like Node
to Node people.

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.460
We have the set of
supported runtimes.

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:14.030
There will be a talk
at this event talking

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:17.210
about a new alpha for our
Ruby runtime for Ruby folks

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:18.780
out there.

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:22.140
And you basically call
GCP APIs directly,

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.970
whereas in the original App
Engine standard runtime, which

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.440
predated the rest
of cloud, there

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.060
were a lot of built-in
APIs for things

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.810
like memcache and other things.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.400
So when you're using
App Engine, you

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:38.430
are primarily thinking
about your code base,

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:41.065
the HTTP requests that
come in, like what URLs

00:12:41.065 --> 00:12:42.690
and where they map,
that sort of thing,

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.510
and different versions of your
app for going forward and back.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:50.390
In fact, you can actually even
break the app up into modules

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:51.330
if you need to.

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:53.840
But that's the
primary mental model

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.660
you're in when you're
using App Engine.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.200
So it's a great fit.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:03.020
Its natural sweet spot is
HTTP web request response

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:04.790
applications.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.790
There's an execution
timeout so you can't

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.490
do really long running things.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.580
But it's meant for these back
and forth request things.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:18.290
It scales very well,
both down to zero

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:19.790
instances of your
back ends running,

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:20.840
all the way to very high.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:23.880
So a lot of folks are
using it in advertising,

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.920
for example, where you have
almost no traffic until an ad

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.150
thing runs, and then it
goes really, really big,

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.143
and then it drops
back off or maybe long

00:13:31.143 --> 00:13:32.060
tails out for a while.

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:33.582
But you need to
leave it up, only

00:13:33.582 --> 00:13:34.790
paying for what you're using.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.100
Again, there are only
certain runtimes available.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:42.710
So if you're running
in those languages,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:44.660
you're going to be happier.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.530
You might not be able
to use it if not.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:50.420
And the window to
the world is the web.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.171
That's how it sees
everything is through HTTP.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.950
And on the flexible
side, it can run anything

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:01.397
that fits in a container,
but it's running on VMs

00:14:01.397 --> 00:14:02.980
and they don't scale
quite as quickly.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.355
So we need to
leave a few running

00:14:04.355 --> 00:14:05.900
so it doesn't scale to zero.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.150
And it takes a little
longer to respond

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.100
to new traffic coming in.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:13.700
By longer, I mean like
minutes-ish, a couple minutes

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.500
kind of territory,
rather than seconds

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:19.370
or sub-second kind
of time frame.

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.070
So you might choose App
Engine because it lets

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.990
you just focus on your code.

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:26.970
Maybe your organization
is primarily developers.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.220
That's what you care about.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:30.663
Or maybe for this
particular app,

00:14:30.663 --> 00:14:32.330
you don't want to
invest the ops into it

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:34.160
because it's not
your core thing, so

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.320
that part of your system.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.262
It's very much optimized
for web-focused workloads.

00:14:40.262 --> 00:14:41.720
And it's especially
good for things

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:45.380
that vary a lot over time.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:49.520
And this stat is
probably out of date,

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:51.050
but I couldn't find
the newest one.

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:53.420
Definitely lots and lots
and lots of traffic on App

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:54.530
Engine as a whole.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.450
And we can handle
the load that you've

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:58.700
got there is the
point to make there.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:07.550
So now, actually,
we've got a new thing.

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:10.670
And there is a talk
later today that

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:14.360
goes into the details of this.

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:15.890
This is Cloud Run.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:17.793
And what we're trying to do--

00:15:17.793 --> 00:15:19.460
if you look around
the internet, there's

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:22.610
a lot of serverless
versus containers

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:24.890
kind of conversations,
like which one do you do?

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:29.075
Like they're in a duel to
the end of time or something.

00:15:29.075 --> 00:15:30.950
And we're trying to
bring these together more

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:34.670
like peanut butter
and jelly or some best

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.500
buddies or something like that.

00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:39.410
So we're trying
to give serverless

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:40.850
kind of agility,
that pay for what

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.130
you use to scale
up and scale down,

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:46.880
to nearly anything you can put
in a container that listens

00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:48.920
on HTTP.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.900
So what it is--

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:55.710
you give it a URL
to an image that

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:59.190
has some code in it that
will listen to HTTP whatever

00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:01.500
the port environment
variable is.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.680
And in return, you get something
that scales up and down

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:05.730
very quickly.

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:07.560
You get the SSL
termination, the HTTP

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:11.980
request binding through all the
load balancers and the like.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:17.600
And really nice
thing for efficiency

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:20.090
is that it's not
just single threaded.

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:22.642
If you're running Java or you're
running Go or something has

00:16:22.642 --> 00:16:24.350
really good concurrency,
you can actually

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:28.820
handle multiple requests from
the same binary execution.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.292
And so if you're running
something that actually

00:16:31.292 --> 00:16:32.750
has to wait on
other network calls,

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.640
you can actually get
a lot of efficiency

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.380
there in what you're paying for.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.310
And scales up and
down really fast.

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.260
And you're paying
very, very precisely

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.690
for the CPU and memory
you're using down

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:47.251
to 100 millisecond granularity.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:53.020
That's the hosted,
fully-managed version.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:54.750
With the same
developer experience,

00:16:54.750 --> 00:17:00.010
there's also a back end that
runs on top of Kubernetes.

00:17:00.010 --> 00:17:02.770
And so you can deploy
things in the same way,

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:06.190
have all the load balancing and
everything set up correctly--

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:08.800
the service endpoints and
everything hooked up--

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:11.050
but it's part of a Kubernetes
cluster you already have

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:15.060
or that you're trying to
build out on your systems.

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.060
It deeply integrates with
the policy and control

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.790
you're already running there.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:21.310
If you need custom
hardware, you need

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:26.089
specific kinds of accelerators
like GPUs or TPUs available,

00:17:26.089 --> 00:17:27.160
you can do that.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:31.630
And because it's running
on top of Kubernetes,

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:33.770
we can actually run
this in a lot of places.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:37.600
So essentially,
we're trying to--

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:40.690
if you're coming from the
serverless angle of not

00:17:40.690 --> 00:17:44.170
thinking about back ends and you
want to just have more options,

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:46.738
think about the fully
managed Cloud Run.

00:17:46.738 --> 00:17:48.280
If you're coming at
this from, I want

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:53.110
to offer serverless
experience to the people who

00:17:53.110 --> 00:17:55.600
are using my Kubernetes
cluster, then

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.530
the running on top
of GKE angle makes

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:01.120
a lot of sense for Cloud Run.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:04.390
So same developer experience,
two different back ends.

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:07.900
You can move between them just
by redeploying the same app.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.660
So you can start one place
and just redeploy to the other

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:12.600
and go.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:17.140
So let's try to make this real.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:18.500
This is the Hello World app.

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:23.290
So we're not going to go
too real, but it is real.

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:24.940
So we're basically-- sorry.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:26.148
Let's start at the beginning.

00:18:26.148 --> 00:18:27.700
We're back at our project top.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.800
Choose Cloud Run.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.840
And then we'll go straight
to Create Service.

