00:21:26.960 --> 00:21:29.070
a sneak on the next little bit.

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:33.140
So when you're using
Cloud Run, you're

00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:35.480
thinking about building
the container environment

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.430
for the processes you have.

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:41.900
So you can run any
language you like.

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:44.360
Anything that will run
on Linux in a Docker

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:48.500
container you can run that
understands how to do HTTP.

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:51.380
So you look for the
environment variable port,

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:53.720
you open a listening
socket on that port,

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:56.370
and you're good to go.

00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:58.935
It is, however,
stateless, in that

00:21:58.935 --> 00:22:01.310
there is a local file system
but it's actually in memory.

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:06.652
And so between invocations of
your app, it just goes away.

00:22:06.652 --> 00:22:08.360
So you can't store a
lot of stuff locally

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.802
and you shouldn't, other than
maybe cache if you have to.

00:22:10.802 --> 00:22:12.260
It's mostly there
for compatibility

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:13.880
with libraries that expect it.

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:16.550
So you should think everything
that comes in and goes out.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:24.380
Let's try to go back and see if
we got a new URL deployed just

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:25.700
now.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:28.230
Moment of truth.

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:30.210
It's running.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:32.680
Yay.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:36.360
Thank you, members
of the audience

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:40.600
and supporting me in this
really alive demo thing.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:44.700
So this is a good fit for
things that are stateless.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.175
Again, HTTP request
response workloads.

00:22:47.175 --> 00:22:49.050
That's how you talk to
it is through the load

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:51.290
balancers over HTTP.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:52.590
It can handle bursty traffic.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:54.270
It scales down all
the way to zero.

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.740
You pay for exactly
what you use.

00:22:56.740 --> 00:23:00.210
And if you have a scenario
where you really need

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:04.038
specific runtimes or languages
or binaries around there--

00:23:04.038 --> 00:23:06.330
because it's not just the
code you're actually writing.

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:09.330
You could include other
things that you shell out to

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:13.650
or whatever you need to
do in that Docker image

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:14.400
to solve problems.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:16.590
If you've got some really
gnarly stuff that's

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:20.460
been around the office forever,
if you can put in a container,

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:24.340
you can put that on the web,
make it feel serverless.

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.460
On the constraint side
it is, again, containers.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:30.310
So it has to be in a
container to run it.

00:23:30.310 --> 00:23:33.570
And because we're getting
a little more focused here,

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:36.430
how you build those
images is up to you.

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:37.675
So it depends.

00:23:37.675 --> 00:23:39.550
You have to decide how
you're going to do it.

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:40.470
We have Cloud Build.

00:23:40.470 --> 00:23:42.780
There's lots of other
CI/CD tools out there.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:46.680
Anything that results
in a Docker image

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:49.680
in a repository that has
the other things like

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:53.730
listens on a port and
stuff you can use in here.

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:55.710
So why would you use it?

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:57.940
If you want this
developer experience,

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:01.270
this kind of serverless
developer experience,

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:06.000
you have the option of having
it be fully managed or deeply

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.800
integrated with Kubernetes.

00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:12.450
And you can decide later at ops
time, even a different group

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:14.880
of people, what that
deployment environment actually

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:15.850
looks like.

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.330
So you can do all of
your dev work in one

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:21.000
and then decide later
to switch to the other

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.710
just by deploying the code.

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:25.920
And you can run
basically anything that

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.382
runs in a container over HTTP.

00:24:28.382 --> 00:24:30.840
And then I've got a new word
that shows up here at the end.

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:35.280
I'm claiming open and
Knative, Kubernetes.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:43.050
So you can actually run
these same images on Knative

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:46.378
on top of Kubernetes anywhere
that Kubernetes runs.

00:24:46.378 --> 00:24:48.420
So if you want to run this
on your own machines--

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:50.250
you've got your own data
center, you're in another cloud,

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.080
you want to run it
in all the places--

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.200
you can take this same
runtime environment

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.405
and run it in all of those
and make that decision

00:24:57.405 --> 00:24:58.530
at deployment time as well.

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:04.140
So there's Cloud Run.

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:05.710
Hopefully you
think it's awesome.

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:07.780
Give it a try.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:11.500
The place we're at right now
is we're just releasing it.

