00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:01.360
So Kubernetes is a
management system

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:03.280
for running lots of containers.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:06.330
So containers give you this
isolation for a process.

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:08.760
It sees the same files
every time it turns on.

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:11.980
Every time you start one up,
they see the same exact state.

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:14.200
So try to solve that, well,
it worked on my machine,

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:16.570
but it doesn't work
over here problem.

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:18.640
And if you want to
do a lot of those,

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.780
you end up wanting to
that on lots of machines.

00:26:20.780 --> 00:26:22.197
And that's what
Kubernetes is for.

00:26:22.197 --> 00:26:24.460
It lets you talk
to a whole cluster

00:26:24.460 --> 00:26:27.352
as if it's kind of
one big machine.

00:26:27.352 --> 00:26:28.810
So you can say,
oh, hey Kubernetes,

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:30.640
I want to run 1,000
of those, and it'll

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:33.010
figure out where to run
them, that sort of thing.

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:34.427
And then a whole
bunch of features

00:26:34.427 --> 00:26:36.700
for making that
relatively easy to manage,

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:39.100
reliable, sensible,
troubleshooting,

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:40.390
all the fun stuff.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.130
And I'll make the claim.

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.640
I think it's become a de
facto way of doing that,

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:48.420
running containers
on lots of machines.

00:26:48.420 --> 00:26:53.430
So Kubernetes Engine is
hosted and managed Kubernetes

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:54.690
on Google Cloud.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.840
So we run the
master node for you.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:01.508
We keep the compute
nodes up to date,

00:27:01.508 --> 00:27:03.300
basically kind of moving
things off of them

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:06.240
and rebooting, creating new ones
if they need security patches

00:27:06.240 --> 00:27:07.950
or what have you.

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:09.630
Do the software
updates for Kubernetes

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:13.800
itself and the underlying
OS, and then do integration

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:16.800
with things the load
balancer, and do cluster auto

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:21.250
scaling to follow the
load that you have.

00:27:21.250 --> 00:27:24.120
So basically makes it easier to
operate a Kubernetes cluster.

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:27.480
And if you're starting
with Kubernetes

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.390
or interested in getting started
with Kubernetes, in my opinion,

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.015
no matter where you
plan on running it,

00:27:32.015 --> 00:27:33.657
this is a really
great place to start.

00:27:33.657 --> 00:27:35.490
Because you can just,
in about five minutes,

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:37.650
have a cluster up and going.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:40.350
You can experiment with
it, see if the model works

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.570
for what you're trying
to do, and go from there.

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:45.413
So when you're using
Kubernetes, you're

00:27:45.413 --> 00:27:47.705
definitely thinking at a
different level of abstraction

00:27:47.705 --> 00:27:48.872
than we talked about so far.

00:27:48.872 --> 00:27:51.000
You're thinking about
how different parts

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:54.250
of your software talk to
other parts of your system.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:57.630
So you might have five
instances of a web front end,

00:27:57.630 --> 00:28:00.660
and one database, and a
couple of cache servers

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:01.900
or something like that.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:05.460
So it starts to feel a little
bit more like a UML diagram,

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:07.410
or a diagram you might
draw on a whiteboard

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:08.880
with your colleagues.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.460
And then we express them
as these YAML files.

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:14.220
But you can actually
take these--

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:17.550
that are computer-oriented
descriptions of that whiteboard

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:18.090
thing--

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:19.548
and push them in
a cluster and have

00:28:19.548 --> 00:28:22.080
it actually create
that structure, which

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.060
is really powerful.

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:27.510
And you're thinking about
the application as a whole,

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:29.220
and hopefully not
thinking much about,

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:31.390
how many computers do I have?

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:32.730
How do they talk to each other?

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:35.280
Because Kubrick and his
managers how the things find

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.940
each other, where the
processes actually run,

00:28:38.940 --> 00:28:40.180
and that sort of thing.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:42.430
So it keeps you thinking
about the logical connections

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:46.830
between your app and
insulates you, in many ways,

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:48.840
from the actual
execution environment.

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:51.760
So you can run it in different
clouds or on your own machines.

00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:54.570
So that's the key
thing in my mind.

00:28:54.570 --> 00:28:56.220
It's a really good
fit for scenarios

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:59.130
where you need to run in
multiple environments.

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:00.750
So you need to have
the same thing run

00:29:00.750 --> 00:29:02.625
on a dev cluster that's
on your own machines,

00:29:02.625 --> 00:29:05.220
but you're going to deploy
it to a cloud or two clouds.

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:09.780
Or you need to
make sure you have

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:12.060
the same environment
for dev, test, and prod,

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:15.240
but you can't have all the
same number of machines,

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:17.890
that sort of thing.

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:20.840
If you really want to take
full advantage of containers,

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:24.570
go all in, there's just a lot
more configurability available

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:27.370
in Kubernetes.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:30.070
It does make the
most sense when you

00:29:30.070 --> 00:29:33.340
have really good communications
with the other teams

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:35.110
in your organization
because you're

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:37.277
going to need to have
conversations about how things

00:29:37.277 --> 00:29:41.560
get built, how security
patches are going to work.

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:43.300
The ops folks and
devs folks agree

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:47.200
on approach and some
conventions in places,

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:49.720
probably that they can all
commit the same repos to make

00:29:49.720 --> 00:29:53.025
changes if they need to.

00:29:53.025 --> 00:29:54.400
And you're probably
going to want

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.700
some sort of build
pipeline system to do

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:01.090
that collaboration in.

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:02.440
And it does give you--

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:04.600
like you have
processes listening

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:07.970
on ports that can talk to
each other via load balancing

00:30:07.970 --> 00:30:08.842
routing.

00:30:08.842 --> 00:30:10.300
Everything I've
talked about so far

00:30:10.300 --> 00:30:12.910
has all been like
HTTP, HTTPS, right?

00:30:12.910 --> 00:30:15.310
So you want to do
other stuff, there's

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:18.970
lots and lots of options for
connecting the pieces here.

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:21.960
Still, you must
be in containers.

00:30:21.960 --> 00:30:24.550
There can be some
challenges around licensing,

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:29.320
where licenses for some apps
are actually per machine.

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:31.542
So you have to do some
pinning or some special things

00:30:31.542 --> 00:30:34.000
with labels in order to make
sure that the processes always

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:36.640
end up on those machines that
you've got the licenses set up

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:38.520
for.

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:40.570
And Kubernetes has
an opinion about how

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:41.710
things talk to each other.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:43.460
And so that can influence
the architecture

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:46.660
that you decide to do.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:50.530
So Kubernetes, Kubernetes
Engine can insulate you

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:52.490
from a lot of infrastructure.

00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:55.810
The reason Google's excited
about a lot of this stuff

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:59.020
is it enables high
utilization, which saves money.

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:02.770
You can run the same stuff
reliably at higher utilization

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:05.320
and not leave a bunch of
open headroom for every app

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:08.350
individually because you've
got a reliable cluster you can

00:31:08.350 --> 00:31:10.100
move the processes around on.

00:31:10.100 --> 00:31:12.730
So you can actually
safely and reliably run

00:31:12.730 --> 00:31:17.980
much higher utilization, which
directly equals save money.

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:19.420
The abstractions
that it provides

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:21.128
are a real sweet spot
for a lot of folks.

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:26.297
And you are going to
have to figure out

00:31:26.297 --> 00:31:28.380
what you're going to do
for logging and monitoring

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:29.880
and how all the
pieces fit together.

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:31.690
So there's that.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:32.190
OK.

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.820
Next level down,
virtual machines.

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:39.820
So now we're in the
territory of we've

00:31:39.820 --> 00:31:43.530
got an operating system of some
kind and everything above that

00:31:43.530 --> 00:31:47.130
is up to you, all
of it, including

00:31:47.130 --> 00:31:48.750
OS patches and the like.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:51.660
Which means you can install
whatever kernel you want

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:55.287
and additional things
you may need to do there.

00:31:55.287 --> 00:31:57.120
If you've got scenarios
where you need that,

00:31:57.120 --> 00:32:00.450
you can do that on these VMs.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:02.430
One thing I want to
highlight is we often

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:05.290
talk about scaling
out in cloud, right?

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:08.010
You know, lots of little
processes and the like.

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:11.140
This is an area they're
getting quite large.

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:13.980
We just last week
announced machines

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:17.120
with up to 12 terabytes of RAM.

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:22.220
And I went to another
company's retail site

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:26.630
and looked for big hard disks
that I could order yesterday.

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:29.550
And the largest I could
find was 14 terabytes.

00:32:29.550 --> 00:32:32.900
So if you want to go order a
hard disk, it's 14 terabytes.

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:35.870
Pretty soon you're going to
be able to just virtually turn

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:39.275
on a machine that has
12 terabytes of memory.

00:32:44.545 --> 00:32:46.670
So yes, there's limits on
how far you can scale up,

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:49.310
but they're probably higher
than we are thinking.

00:32:54.150 --> 00:32:57.540
They start very quickly,
usually around 20 seconds.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:00.030
We have a bunch of
pre-built OS images

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:03.857
that are managed
in the sense of we

00:33:03.857 --> 00:33:05.940
make sure that they've got
stuff installed on them

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:08.580
that makes it easy
to use Google Cloud.

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:13.200
You can get the On OS
Update systems kind of go

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:16.320
through repo mirrors
that we have.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:20.842
So as security patches happen,
it all happens fairly locally.

00:33:20.842 --> 00:33:22.050
So consider those if you can.

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:26.250
But you can actually make a
block device that is bootable,

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:28.680
and upload that
and boot off of it.

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:31.920
So you can do all the way
down to running whatever

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:36.580
you want on these machines.

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:37.751
I want to highlight--

00:33:40.855 --> 00:33:41.980
these are virtual machines.

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:44.600
They're CPU, network,
disk and the like.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:47.060
But a lot of the pieces
are not so intuitive.

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:48.482
So the disks are most--

00:33:48.482 --> 00:33:50.440
the persistent disk,
which is our default disk,

00:33:50.440 --> 00:33:51.820
is a network attached storage.

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.290
So the block device
is actually spread out

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:59.380
on thousands of machines.

00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:02.860
And they actually compete
to return the data back.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:06.320
So the random read that you
expect from spinning disk,

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:09.310
even though it's spinning disk,
it's actually lots of them.

00:34:09.310 --> 00:34:11.679
So you get a really
flat latency instead

00:34:11.679 --> 00:34:13.719
of a spiky latency on reads.

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:17.510
And it's faster than
you would think.

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:20.949
And because these pages are all
spread out all over the place,

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:23.330
you can actually take
a snapshot of the disk

00:34:23.330 --> 00:34:27.100
while it's connected with almost
no interruption on the machine.

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:28.630
There's just a
little blip where you

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:31.600
need to have a lock for a
couple of milliseconds to say,

00:34:31.600 --> 00:34:34.938
this is the most recent
block that's there.

00:34:34.938 --> 00:34:37.480
And then behind the scenes, it
can actually copy that all off

00:34:37.480 --> 00:34:39.207
and make a snapshot
of the full disk

00:34:39.207 --> 00:34:41.290
that you can then restore
to make another computer

00:34:41.290 --> 00:34:44.590
with exactly that data on it.

00:34:44.590 --> 00:34:47.332
And you can do that anywhere,
in any of the zones we have.

00:34:47.332 --> 00:34:48.790
So you can get a
snapshot in the US

00:34:48.790 --> 00:34:50.600
and restore it in
Asia or Europe,

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:52.270
or wherever, and do that.

00:34:56.728 --> 00:34:58.270
I mentioned to
somebody earlier, that

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:00.760
leads to some
non-intuitive effects

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:05.040
that larger disks are faster.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.150
Wait, what?

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:10.290
So bigger disks are
actually faster.

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:14.808
So if you're doing tests
of speed on these VMs,

00:35:14.808 --> 00:35:16.600
look at the docs and
look at the trade-offs

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:18.225
because you need to
make sure your disk

00:35:18.225 --> 00:35:19.940
is fast enough for
the IOPS you want.

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:22.820
And the reason for
that is that disks

00:35:22.820 --> 00:35:27.790
come with a combined amount
of I/O and storage on them,

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:29.510
and put those on
the data center.

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:31.010
And we want to be
fair to people who

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:33.230
are trying to get
all the IOPS possible

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:36.540
and other folks who are trying
to store big piles of data.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.140
So the two are
actually linked up.

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:41.690
So if you want the fastest
throughput on the disks,

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:46.235
you need larger
disks and vise versa.

00:35:46.235 --> 00:35:47.610
I guess you want
lots of storage,

00:35:47.610 --> 00:35:49.680
you need larger disks too.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:54.320
So pay attention to that when
you go do performance testing.

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:56.188
And the other piece.

00:35:56.188 --> 00:35:58.230
These machines come and
go really quickly, right?

00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.660
20 seconds, the like.

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:04.720
You don't need Kubernetes
to get auto scaling.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:06.970
You can actually make what's
called a managed instance

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:09.250
group of identical--

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:11.080
you make a VM template.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.390
And then it will scale up
and down multiple machines,

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:17.050
load balance across
them, and do that based

00:36:17.050 --> 00:36:21.580
on the traffic you have or a
value you can set in the API.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:25.960
So you can actually scale up a
cluster and down very easily.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:29.140
If you're trying to build
a disaster recovery site,

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:31.048
don't leave all of them
running all the time.

00:36:31.048 --> 00:36:33.340
Have a managed instance group
at the front ends running

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:34.975
one really small one.

00:36:34.975 --> 00:36:36.850
And then, when the
disaster actually happens,

00:36:36.850 --> 00:36:38.750
then spin up all the machines.

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:40.970
You save so much money.

00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:42.820
It's really easy to
think of these as a one

00:36:42.820 --> 00:36:44.050
to one equivalents.

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:45.550
And I want to
encourage you to think

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:48.477
of them as being very elastic.

00:36:48.477 --> 00:36:50.310
And that's true for the
individual machines.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:51.852
So you have these
groups of machines,

00:36:51.852 --> 00:36:55.540
but I mentioned
these rates, right?

00:36:55.540 --> 00:36:58.150
You can actually mix and
match and have a lot of memory

00:36:58.150 --> 00:37:01.090
and very little CPU
or the opposite.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:03.550
And you can start with a
machine that's very, very small,

00:37:03.550 --> 00:37:09.590
stop it, change the settings
and reboot it to have 160 VCPUs.

00:37:09.590 --> 00:37:11.950
So it starts with two when
you're like, OK, just reboot.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:13.300
Now I've got 100.

00:37:13.300 --> 00:37:16.150
So it's very, very elastic.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:18.110
Take advantage of that.

00:37:18.110 --> 00:37:21.580
So when you're using virtual
machines, using Compute Engine,

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:23.230
you're thinking
about your software,

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:24.700
what's running on the machine.

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:26.450
You're thinking about
the operating system

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:30.650
itself, the disk images,
the CPU, RAM, disk ratios,

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:32.840
the networking, how
they talk to each other.

00:37:32.840 --> 00:37:34.330
There's a lot more
moving pieces.

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:37.680
But you have control
over all of them.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:39.940
There's cases where you
really want and need that.

00:37:39.940 --> 00:37:42.850
So it's all there.

00:37:42.850 --> 00:37:46.670
So it's a really, really, really
good fit for existing systems.

00:37:46.670 --> 00:37:49.098
So it's almost always
the right first step

00:37:49.098 --> 00:37:50.890
for a system that
already exists and you're

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:54.642
trying to get some
advantages of cloud from it.

00:37:54.642 --> 00:37:56.350
This is almost always
the place to start.

00:37:56.350 --> 00:37:58.930
You'll get a lot of
benefits just moving

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:01.840
the machines over, things like
these snapshots to take backups

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:05.050
and the like, the networking.

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:07.240
There is a possibility.

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:08.740
All I want to do
is run a container.

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:09.823
I just got this one image.

00:38:09.823 --> 00:38:11.620
I just want to run it once.

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.680
As you're starting a VM, you
can actually click and say,

00:38:14.680 --> 00:38:16.900
I want to run this container.

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:18.940
And we'll start a
container optimized OS

00:38:18.940 --> 00:38:23.200
and start that one container
and just do the one.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:26.890
If you have needs for a
specific kernel or OS version,

00:38:26.890 --> 00:38:28.900
particular license
requirements, probably

00:38:28.900 --> 00:38:30.358
if you're trying
to run a database,

00:38:30.358 --> 00:38:32.052
you're going to be happier here.

00:38:32.052 --> 00:38:34.010
It's possible to do in
some of the other things

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:36.910
but there's a lot
more moving parts.

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:39.850
And then, again, if you've got
network protocols beyond HTTP,

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:42.770
HTTPS.

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:44.080
The scaling is more granular.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:46.790
It's still pretty
quick in the big terms.

00:38:46.790 --> 00:38:51.070
But it takes 20, 30
seconds to set up a VM.

00:38:51.070 --> 00:38:54.220
You can start lots of
them in a minute or two.

00:38:54.220 --> 00:38:55.810
But there's a granularity there.

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:57.917
If you're trying to do
really spiky workloads,

00:38:57.917 --> 00:39:00.000
it might not keep up, and
you want to look higher.

00:39:03.910 --> 00:39:07.045
I'm going to skip most of
the demo but I can't resist.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:11.440
There we go.

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:20.050
And basically, the way this
looks is you pick a name.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:27.640
You choose the kind of balance
of preset number of CPUs

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:29.410
versus memory.

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:31.810
Or click Customize and
you get these sliders.

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:37.990
And you're like, oh, I
want this shape machine.

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:40.240
And I'll pick a different
disk and that sort of thing.

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:42.198
So I'm not going to
actually do this and log in

00:39:42.198 --> 00:39:44.905
because I'm trying to say
too many things in one thing.

00:39:44.905 --> 00:39:47.830
But I wanted to highlight
down here at the bottom--

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:49.930
and this is true for
a lot of the UI--

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:54.940
there is this little thing that
says, oh, what's the equivalent

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:56.800
REST or command line?

00:39:56.800 --> 00:39:59.470
And this will give you the
actual JSON that the web app is

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:02.890
going to send to the API to
do what you do when you click,

00:40:02.890 --> 00:40:08.770
or the G Cloud command
to do the same thing.

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:10.770
So if you explore a flow
that you like and you

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:12.420
want to automate
that, you can actually

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:14.490
copy this out, put
it in a script,

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:18.390
put it on a scheduled
task, and go.

00:40:18.390 --> 00:40:23.430
So it's a really powerful way to
kind of learn your way around.

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:26.040
So why Compute Engine?

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:28.050
You want consistency.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:33.650
You should be able
get the same behavior.

00:40:33.650 --> 00:40:35.570
I stopped and somebody
laughed-- hey--

00:40:35.570 --> 00:40:40.010
the same behavior in test
and then later in prod.

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:42.350
You get these very custom VMs.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:44.390
You can actually-- I
didn't even say it.

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:49.040
Because these disks are
virtual block devices,

00:40:49.040 --> 00:40:50.870
you can actually
make them larger

00:40:50.870 --> 00:40:52.410
while the machine is running.

00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:54.260
So anybody run out
of a disk before

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:56.790
and had a machine go down?

00:40:56.790 --> 00:41:00.480
You can actually make the
disk bigger while it's on.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:02.080
It's really powerful.

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:03.570
We have a lot of
things we've done

00:41:03.570 --> 00:41:06.280
in billing to try to make it
really easy to understand.

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:12.590
So it's basically billed in
small increments, like seconds.

00:41:12.590 --> 00:41:14.540
And if you just leave
it on, we automatically

00:41:14.540 --> 00:41:16.520
give you discounts.

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:18.050
So there's up to
like a 30% discount

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:19.770
if it runs for a whole month.

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:24.290
And buy "it," I mean number
of CPUs and I'm out of memory

00:41:24.290 --> 00:41:27.050
on the same kind of general
machinist shape, not

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:27.960
specific machines.

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:31.090
So you can restart them and that
sort of thing and pay there.

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:34.010
And we have this thing
called preemptible machines

00:41:34.010 --> 00:41:39.110
that are our attempt to get
you to think really flexibly.

00:41:39.110 --> 00:41:42.590
Because they're
up to 80% discount

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:44.240
of the price of a regular VM.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:46.440
They're the same VMs.

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:49.890
But they could be stopped within
30 to 60 seconds at any time

00:41:49.890 --> 00:41:52.960
and they will not live
more than 24 hours.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:57.000
So it encourages you to think
about writing your tools

00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:59.700
in terms of jobs that
come in, do some work,

00:41:59.700 --> 00:42:01.660
move on, that sort of thing.

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:03.540
And if you do, you
can just immediately

00:42:03.540 --> 00:42:08.880
save a quarter to a fifth of
the overall price, just boom.

00:42:08.880 --> 00:42:09.880
It goes down to that.

00:42:09.880 --> 00:42:13.582
So it's 80% off,
like 70% to 80% off.

00:42:13.582 --> 00:42:14.790
Look at preemptible machines.

00:42:14.790 --> 00:42:17.970
It's totally worth it.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:18.880
But it will turn off.

00:42:18.880 --> 00:42:21.360
So make your stuff so that
it reboots and works off

00:42:21.360 --> 00:42:22.080
cues and things.

00:42:24.820 --> 00:42:26.760
And we use Compute
Engine to build

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:27.870
a lot of our own services.

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:29.970
So a lot of folks here,
we're talking about data.

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:32.010
So Dataflow and
Dataproc are actually

00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:34.170
built on top of Compute
Engine instances.

00:42:34.170 --> 00:42:39.090
The managed SQL services
we have actually run

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:41.792
on top of Compute instances.

00:42:41.792 --> 00:42:43.500
These are very powerful
and really strong

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:45.208
building blocks for
your systems as well.

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:50.720
I promised to answer
this question, right?

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:52.762
We've mostly just talked
about a bunch of things.

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:56.970
So we're back to here.

00:42:56.970 --> 00:42:59.470
Hopefully these make more sense
than they did a little while

00:42:59.470 --> 00:42:59.970
ago.

00:43:03.530 --> 00:43:06.700
If, in hearing this, you're
like, oh, that's it, great.

00:43:06.700 --> 00:43:08.380
Start there.

00:43:08.380 --> 00:43:12.340
But probably we're still in
"it depends" a little bit.

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:13.840
So here are some
additional thoughts

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:17.995
to think about that may
push you up or down in

00:43:17.995 --> 00:43:19.120
between these abstractions.

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:21.317
So you may have a
different abstraction

00:43:21.317 --> 00:43:22.400
level you want to work at.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:24.483
There may be some technical
requirements you have,

00:43:24.483 --> 00:43:27.040
or some things about your
team, the people you work with,

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:30.290
that might make you want
to choose one or the other.

00:43:30.290 --> 00:43:32.920
So thinking in terms
of abstractions--

00:43:32.920 --> 00:43:35.050
this is just summarizing
what I said there--

00:43:35.050 --> 00:43:38.920
you're thinking about events
and functions, or just

00:43:38.920 --> 00:43:42.790
your code base, or
the container format,

00:43:42.790 --> 00:43:48.940
or the UML diagram of the app,
or the actual representation

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:50.110
of a machine.

00:43:50.110 --> 00:43:52.480
So what abstraction do
you want to be working at?

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:56.180
And it might be different
for different problems.

00:43:56.180 --> 00:43:57.650
You may have
technical requirements

00:43:57.650 --> 00:43:59.650
that are going to push
you one way or the other.

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.730
And in case, usually
down, like the stack.

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:07.120
So you're usually going to need
to go below a given abstraction

00:44:07.120 --> 00:44:07.620
level.

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:10.940
So if you need direct
control over URL routing,

00:44:10.940 --> 00:44:13.243
you want to share
code more easily,

00:44:13.243 --> 00:44:15.410
have more control of versions
and traffic splitting,

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:19.040
that'll bring you from
functions to App Engine.

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:21.290
If you need very specific
programming languages

00:44:21.290 --> 00:44:23.577
or environments and
the like, that's

00:44:23.577 --> 00:44:25.910
going to bring you more towards
setting up the container

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:27.980
to run in Cloud Run.

00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:31.280
If you need specific
things from your OS--

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:35.030
you're doing hybrid work,
network protocols beyond HTTP,

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:37.220
that'll bring you
probably to Kubernetes.

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:39.768
And then you want to just
basically own the whole thing,

00:44:39.768 --> 00:44:40.810
that'll bring you to VMs.

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:47.900
There's also billing model
differences between these.

00:44:47.900 --> 00:44:52.020
So how you plan for paying
for them and how it works.

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:56.130
So Functions, App Engine,
and Managed Cloud Run

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:59.310
are very granular,
pay for actual use

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:03.590
of the CPU and memory,
network involved in doing

00:45:03.590 --> 00:45:06.290
that specific transaction.

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:12.350
Cloud Run on GKE, GKE itself,
so Kubernetes and VMs,

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:14.570
you're standing up
a set of resources

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:16.543
and paying for them
while they're on.

00:45:16.543 --> 00:45:18.960
And then when you turn them
off, you stop paying for them.

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:21.500
So there's a different
model for how

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:24.650
you pay for it, how you
plan for that payment

00:45:24.650 --> 00:45:27.510
and predict what it's going
to cost, how you do that.

00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:30.050
So that may affect
your decisions.

00:45:30.050 --> 00:45:31.460
The teams you're working on.

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:34.927
Is it a group of all devs
working on a new thing?

00:45:34.927 --> 00:45:37.010
That's going to have you
in a very different place

00:45:37.010 --> 00:45:44.420
than we're trying to keep this
stuff working and we have to.

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:46.910
My favorite definition
of Enterprise

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:50.570
is it's any environment where
most of the applications

00:45:50.570 --> 00:45:54.080
don't have an application
team working on them.

00:45:54.080 --> 00:45:56.310
You've got a whole bunch
of stuff that has to run,

00:45:56.310 --> 00:45:58.370
but you can't change them.

00:45:58.370 --> 00:46:01.370
So that's going to probably
have you start more

00:46:01.370 --> 00:46:04.590
towards the Compute
Engine side of things.

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:07.640
So some balances there.

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:10.415
If you're trying to find a
mapping of other things--

00:46:10.415 --> 00:46:12.290
I attempted to make a
map of how you might do

00:46:12.290 --> 00:46:13.910
similar things in other places.

00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:19.970
So functions as a
service, there's lots of--

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:22.460
I think all the clouds and lots
of open source environments

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:25.730
have a way to run functions.

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:27.860
I struggled a bit to
find a clear equivalent

00:46:27.860 --> 00:46:33.140
for App Engine, maybe something
like Cloud Foundry or AppScale,

00:46:33.140 --> 00:46:38.150
which is an open source App
Engine like environment.

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:40.760
Cloud Run is basically--

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:42.260
the UX is a little
bit different,

00:46:42.260 --> 00:46:44.800
but the execution environment
is Knative and Istio

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:45.810
on Kubernetes.

00:46:45.810 --> 00:46:48.140
So you can run that
same stuff there.

00:46:48.140 --> 00:46:50.570
You can move those
workloads very easily.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:52.220
Kubernetes is Kubernetes.

00:46:52.220 --> 00:46:55.150
So the environment around it is
different, like what you call,

00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:57.110
but like the same
stuff should work

00:46:57.110 --> 00:46:59.990
wherever you can run it there.

00:46:59.990 --> 00:47:02.090
And then, basically,
there's lots and lots

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:04.580
of options for running whole
OSes on virtual machines.

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:08.660
So some of these are easier to
do hybrid or multi environments

00:47:08.660 --> 00:47:10.820
than others.

00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:13.150
So back to making
the right decision

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:14.640
and kind of no worries.

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:18.310
Hopefully, this feels a
little more comfortable.

00:47:18.310 --> 00:47:21.100
So you can definitely
use them together.

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:23.440
Most real systems use
multiples at once.

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:26.560
It's not like an all or
nothing kind of thing.

00:47:26.560 --> 00:47:30.640
We have tools that make this
a lot easier and better.

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:31.983
I won't go into details.

00:47:31.983 --> 00:47:33.400
The photos here
are probably good.

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:35.530
But Pub/Sub allows
you to send messages

00:47:35.530 --> 00:47:36.790
between all the things.

00:47:36.790 --> 00:47:41.590
Storage lets you store
arbitrary blobs, like data,

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:43.060
separate from all the things.

00:47:43.060 --> 00:47:47.742
Cloud Tasks are like a queue
of things that need doing

00:47:47.742 --> 00:47:49.700
and you want to make sure
that they all happen.

00:47:49.700 --> 00:47:51.580
So transactional
queueing kind of things.

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:54.500
And Scheduler is Cron
in the cloud, basically.

00:47:54.500 --> 00:47:58.730
So at this time, run that and do
that every week kind of thing.

00:47:58.730 --> 00:48:01.220
So those all help you
glue the pieces together.

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:03.490
And you can move
up and down these.

00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:04.180
I'll be honest.

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:07.840
It's easier to move down
in terms of human effort

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.160
than it is to move up.

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:11.440
So if you get things
into containers,

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:14.800
that gives you more options,
and you can move back and forth

00:48:14.800 --> 00:48:16.927
more easily.

00:48:16.927 --> 00:48:18.760
And we're trying to
make the environments as

00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:19.792
similar as possible.

00:48:19.792 --> 00:48:21.250
From all of these
environments, you

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:23.620
can hit a thing called
the metadata server

00:48:23.620 --> 00:48:27.130
and get tokens for the
scope of access rights

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:28.420
you have around Google Cloud.

00:48:28.420 --> 00:48:31.930
So you can always look to
there to get your security

00:48:31.930 --> 00:48:34.090
token for what
you're allowed to do,

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:35.590
whatever the environment is.

00:48:35.590 --> 00:48:37.900
You can use Stackdriver
from all of them.

00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:39.910
We've just today
announced VPC access.

00:48:39.910 --> 00:48:42.040
So you can have your App
Engine and Cloud Functions

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:45.040
show up on the 10-Dot network
of your VMs [? and not-- ?]

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:47.200
like do the public
internet loop.

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:49.880
We're working on
that for Cloud Run.

00:48:49.880 --> 00:48:52.090
And here we are back here.

00:48:52.090 --> 00:48:53.987
So my claim is if
you've got something

00:48:53.987 --> 00:48:55.570
that looks like
these, that's probably

00:48:55.570 --> 00:48:59.720
the place to start evaluating
and then look at those options.

00:48:59.720 --> 00:49:01.870
So thank you all
very, very, very much.

00:49:01.870 --> 00:49:04.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

